    ('026c8927-4c95-54a3-8685-5e10dfae53aa', 'acb55e37-bc58-5593-83f6-54843ff48fe9', $q$What must be true about an array before binary search can be used on it?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["It must be sorted", "It must contain no duplicate values", "Its length must be a power of two", "It must contain only positive numbers"]$o$, $a$It must be sorted$a$, $e$Binary search discards half the range by comparing with the middle, which is only valid when the data is ordered. Duplicates are fine and length and sign do not matter.$e$, 10, 1, now(), now()),
    ('043d6f7a-b5eb-54d4-913c-f286a4dfa247', 'acb55e37-bc58-5593-83f6-54843ff48fe9', $q$Roughly how many comparisons does binary search need, at most, on a sorted array of 1000 elements?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["500", "10", "100", "1000"]$o$, $a$10$a$, $e$Each step halves the range, and log2(1000) is just under 10, so about 10 comparisons suffice. 500 or 1000 describes a linear scan.$e$, 10, 2, now(), now()),
