    ('5f6518f2-562a-5ac3-a0de-5773d28e7263', 'acb55e37-bc58-5593-83f6-54843ff48fe9', $q$A sorted array has millions of values, possibly repeated. For many queries you need the FIRST position whose value is >= x. What is best?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["A linear scan from the start for each query", "Copy and sort the array for every query, then scan", "Put the values in a HashSet and look up x", "Binary search that keeps narrowing to the left half even after a match (lower bound)"]$o$, $a$Binary search that keeps narrowing to the left half even after a match (lower bound)$a$, $e$The array is already sorted, so each query is O(log n); continuing left after a match finds the first position. A HashSet cannot answer 'first position >= x', and rescanning or re-sorting wastes time.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
