    ('db8ed1a1-fa30-5f28-829b-3b617b90d9dd', '17fc9818-f17e-5236-9df5-91aab17bf318', $q$For each of 100000 daily temperatures you must find how many days until a warmer day. Which approach is best?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["A monotonic stack of indices in a single pass", "For each day scan forward until a warmer day is found", "Sort the temperatures and binary search each day", "A HashMap from temperature to its day number"]$o$, $a$A monotonic stack of indices in a single pass$a$, $e$The stack gives O(n) total. The forward scan is O(n^2) on a non-increasing input, which is too slow at this size. Sorting loses the day order the answer depends on.$e$, 15, 3, now(), now()),
    ('b2546b24-86b5-5a19-97e1-ef139db191d3', '17fc9818-f17e-5236-9df5-91aab17bf318', $q$Why is the monotonic stack scan O(n) even though a while loop sits inside the for loop?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["The inner loop runs at most twice per element", "The inner loop only runs when the array is sorted", "It is really O(n^2) in the worst case, such as a decreasing array", "Each element is pushed once and popped at most once, so total pops are at most n"]$o$, $a$Each element is pushed once and popped at most once, so total pops are at most n$a$, $e$Work is counted across the whole scan, not per iteration: n pushes plus at most n pops. A decreasing array causes no pops at all, so it is not a worst case.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
