    ('ac06e2b8-298c-5203-bf3a-6faf30ca4752', 'c45bd3e4-393c-5264-8679-9e97b0d12f3a', $q$A road map has positive travel times on each road. You need the fastest time from your home to every other town. Which approach fits?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["Dijkstra with a priority queue", "Plain BFS counting the number of roads", "DFS exploring every path and keeping the smallest", "Sorting the roads by time and taking the cheapest first"]$o$, $a$Dijkstra with a priority queue$a$, $e$Dijkstra respects different edge weights. BFS minimises the number of edges, not total time, and trying all DFS paths is exponential.$e$, 15, 3, now(), now()),
    ('f9db6323-4366-5961-ae13-30a1e1d0448f', 'c45bd3e4-393c-5264-8679-9e97b0d12f3a', $q$What is the running time of Dijkstra with a binary-heap priority queue on a graph with V vertices and E edges?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["O(V + E)", "O(V * E)", "O(E log E + V^2)", "O((V + E) log V)"]$o$, $a$O((V + E) log V)$a$, $e$Each edge may cause a heap insert costing O(log V), plus vertex pops. O(V + E) is BFS, which cannot handle weights.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
    ('e31ba73a-e418-57e3-adf9-bfb7c98bb4c6', 'c45bd3e4-393c-5264-8679-9e97b0d12f3a', $q$Edges: A->B weight 4, A->C weight 5, C->B weight -3. Dijkstra from A finalises B when popping it. What does it report for B?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["2, because it will notice the negative edge", "4, and that is the true shortest distance", "4, but the true shortest distance is 2 via C", "It reports infinity for B"]$o$, $a$4, but the true shortest distance is 2 via C$a$, $e$B is popped with distance 4 before C is processed, and it is never revisited, so the cheaper route A-C-B (5 - 3 = 2) is missed. This is exactly why negative edges are not allowed.$e$, 20, 5, now(), now()),
    ('0fd2d9f5-8d73-5f8a-963c-57f2176ee820', '65b8225b-d588-530d-93fc-26cc7c069223', $q$What is memoization?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["Sorting the inputs so recursion runs faster", "Converting recursion into a loop by hand", "Using more threads to compute subproblems in parallel", "Caching the result of each subproblem the first time it is computed so repeats are looked up"]$o$, $a$Caching the result of each subproblem the first time it is computed so repeats are looked up$a$, $e$A memo table stores answers keyed by the subproblem, so overlapping calls cost O(1) after the first. Writing a loop with a table is tabulation, a different (bottom-up) technique.$e$, 10, 1, now(), now()),
    ('eee0b85d-6a4b-50a5-9987-25156474f786', '65b8225b-d588-530d-93fc-26cc7c069223', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

public class Main {
    static int calls = 0;
    static int fib(int n) {
        calls++;
        if (n < 2) return n;
        return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(fib(5));
        System.out.println(calls);
    }
}
$q$, 'PREDICT_OUTPUT', $o$["5 / 5", "5 / 15", "5 / 10", "8 / 15"]$o$, $a$5 / 15$a$, $e$fib(5) is 5, but plain recursion recomputes the same values: the call count is 15. Counting only 5 calls ignores the repeated subproblems that memoization removes.$e$, 10, 2, now(), now()),
    ('a8ea52b7-89d0-5a69-a989-8bab35e5d547', '65b8225b-d588-530d-93fc-26cc7c069223', $q$You must count the paths through a 30 x 30 grid moving only right or down. The plain recursion is far too slow. What is the best fix?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["Memoize the paths count for each (row, col) cell", "Run the same recursion in several threads", "Use a bigger int type for the counter", "Sort the cells before recursing"]$o$, $a$Memoize the paths count for each (row, col) cell$a$, $e$Paths from a cell depend only on that cell, and many routes reach the same cell, so caching by (row, col) gives 900 states. Threads or a larger type do not remove the repeated work.$e$, 15, 3, now(), now()),
    ('70db1d97-e6ce-5881-aba1-f0b7ccf2a8ec', '65b8225b-d588-530d-93fc-26cc7c069223', $q$With a memo table, what is the time complexity of computing fib(n) recursively?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["O(2^n), the same as plain recursion", "O(log n)", "O(n), because each value is computed once", "O(n^2), because of the memo lookups"]$o$, $a$O(n), because each value is computed once$a$, $e$Each of the n states is computed once and then looked up in O(1). Without the memo the same recursion is exponential.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
    ('d8efe40f-6ef6-5a49-992f-e0e46db6133a', '65b8225b-d588-530d-93fc-26cc7c069223', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

public class Main {
    static int calls = 0;
    static long[] memo = new long[10];
    static long fib(int n) {
        calls++;
        if (n < 2) return n;
        if (memo[n] != 0) return memo[n];
        memo[n] = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
        return memo[n];
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        fib(6);
        System.out.println(calls);
    }
}
