    ('804546aa-e8c0-5387-b98d-b298164a1fd5', '2a96edc3-e83b-5b51-b0d1-447aecea6d35', $q$You need the minimum coins for an amount of 1000000, and a recursive memoized solution would nest a million calls deep. Which is safer?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["The same recursion but with a bigger memo array", "Plain recursion without any cache", "A bottom-up table filled with a loop from 0 up to the amount", "A greedy algorithm that always takes the largest coin"]$o$, $a$A bottom-up table filled with a loop from 0 up to the amount$a$, $e$A loop has no deep call stack, so it cannot overflow, and it still does O(amount * coins) work. A larger memo does not shorten the recursion depth and greedy is not correct for every coin set.$e$, 15, 3, now(), now()),
    ('3f78f968-5d61-5aae-8736-44696ab2a7c3', '2a96edc3-e83b-5b51-b0d1-447aecea6d35', $q$For min-coin change with an amount A and k coin values, what are the time and space of the standard tabulation?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["Time O(A * k), space O(A)", "Time O(A), space O(A * k)", "Time O(k^A), space O(A)", "Time O(A^2), space O(1)"]$o$, $a$Time O(A * k), space O(A)$a$, $e$There are A + 1 table cells and each tries k coins. The exponential bound belongs to brute-force recursion without a table.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
    ('2e064ec8-1be0-547b-b4d9-8f7f22e901e9', '2a96edc3-e83b-5b51-b0d1-447aecea6d35', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String a = "ABCBD", b = "BDCB";
        int[][] dp = new int[a.length() + 1][b.length() + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= a.length(); i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= b.length(); j++)
                dp[i][j] = a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)
                    ? dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
                    : Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
        System.out.println(dp[a.length()][b.length()]);
    }
}
