$q$, 'PREDICT_OUTPUT', $o$["5 / 3616", "4 / 384", "3 / 3616", "4 / 3616"]$o$, $a$4 / 3616$a$, $e$Page number = address / page size = 20000 / 4096 = 4 (integer division). Offset = address % page size = 20000 - 4 * 4096 = 3616. Rounding up to page 5 is the common mistake.$e$, 15, 2, now(), now()),
    ('21a8dd85-0116-5461-9d90-b26fba0ab179', 'd6f843fd-c74a-5b99-aadb-b34dcd0b1fe3', $q$What does the TLB (translation lookaside buffer) cache?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["Recently used page-table entries (page to frame translations)", "Recently used data blocks from main memory", "Pages that were swapped out to disk", "The list of free frames"]$o$, $a$Recently used page-table entries (page to frame translations)$a$, $e$A TLB hit avoids an extra memory access to read the page table. Caching actual data blocks is the job of the CPU data cache, which is a different structure.$e$, 15, 3, now(), now()),
    ('c5cf0b68-fc55-52a6-8317-35583ad06b1b', 'd6f843fd-c74a-5b99-aadb-b34dcd0b1fe3', $q$A 64-bit process uses only a few small regions of its huge virtual address space. A flat page table per process would be enormous. What is the standard remedy?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["Use a hierarchical (multi-level) page table", "Make the page size 1 byte", "Keep one flat table and store it only on disk", "Disable virtual memory for that process"]$o$, $a$Use a hierarchical (multi-level) page table$a$, $e$A multi-level table only allocates second-level tables for regions actually in use, so unused address space costs almost nothing. A 1-byte page would make the table even larger.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
    ('74d99502-44d6-5d52-9a06-3a4297971998', 'd6f843fd-c74a-5b99-aadb-b34dcd0b1fe3', $q$A process touches a valid page that is not currently in RAM. Which sequence describes what happens?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["The process is killed with a segmentation fault", "The OS ignores it and returns zeros", "The OS loads the page into a frame, updates the page table and restarts the instruction", "The OS copies the whole process to disk and restarts it"]$o$, $a$The OS loads the page into a frame, updates the page table and restarts the instruction$a$, $e$This is a page fault: the page is legal but not resident, so the OS fetches it from disk (evicting another page if no frame is free) and retries. A segfault happens only for an illegal access.$e$, 20, 5, now(), now()),
    ('86ac92be-2be7-55e8-8349-409ab903a00c', '5abc8166-ec85-5fd3-bb3d-fa986f6bce78', $q$The optimal page-replacement algorithm (OPT) gives the fewest page faults. Why is it not used in real systems?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["It needs future knowledge of the reference string", "It needs too much disk space", "It only works with two frames", "It causes Belady's anomaly"]$o$, $a$It needs future knowledge of the reference string$a$, $e$OPT evicts the page that will not be used for the longest time, which the OS cannot know in advance. It is used only as a benchmark. OPT never suffers Belady's anomaly.$e$, 10, 1, now(), now()),
    ('c3bbc895-4c06-5c7b-b694-b7f1e8e27489', '5abc8166-ec85-5fd3-bb3d-fa986f6bce78', $q$Which algorithm can produce MORE page faults after you give the process MORE frames (Belady's anomaly)?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["LRU", "OPT", "FIFO", "All stack algorithms"]$o$, $a$FIFO$a$, $e$FIFO ignores recency, so a larger memory can hold a different set of pages and cause extra faults. LRU and OPT are stack algorithms: the pages held with n frames are always a subset of those held with n+1.$e$, 15, 2, now(), now()),
    ('56a34250-bc2f-510e-954a-a814139627cc', '5abc8166-ec85-5fd3-bb3d-fa986f6bce78', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] ref = {1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        for (int f = 3; f <= 4; f++) {
            Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
            int faults = 0;
            for (int p : ref) {
                if (!q.contains(p)) {
                    faults++;
                    if (q.size() == f) q.poll();
                    q.add(p);
                }
            }
            System.out.println(faults);
        }
    }
}
$q$, 'PREDICT_OUTPUT', $o$["9 / 9", "10 / 9", "9 / 10", "10 / 8"]$o$, $a$9 / 10$a$, $e$With 3 frames FIFO faults 9 times, with 4 frames it faults 10 times. This is the textbook Belady's anomaly: more frames, more faults.$e$, 15, 3, now(), now()),
    ('8a0252da-fcf9-55c9-907a-b0a848c5810e', '5abc8166-ec85-5fd3-bb3d-fa986f6bce78', $q$Exact LRU needs a timestamp or stack update on every memory reference, which is too slow. What do real systems use instead?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["Evict a random frame every time", "Always evict the most recently loaded page", "Evict the page with the lowest page number", "An approximation such as the clock (second-chance) algorithm using reference bits"]$o$, $a$An approximation such as the clock (second-chance) algorithm using reference bits$a$, $e$The hardware sets a reference bit on each access and the clock hand gives recently used pages a second chance, approximating LRU cheaply. Random or lowest-number eviction ignores locality.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
    ('1f42909e-e884-5a9f-a93d-9f69d9ce8bc1', '5abc8166-ec85-5fd3-bb3d-fa986f6bce78', $q$CPU utilization is falling and the disk is constantly paging. The OS reacts by admitting more processes. Why does this make things worse?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["More processes take frames away from the others, increasing page faults and deepening thrashing", "More processes always improve CPU utilization", "The disk gets faster under heavier load", "Page faults stop once more processes are running"]$o$, $a$More processes take frames away from the others, increasing page faults and deepening thrashing$a$, $e$Thrashing means processes lack enough frames for their working sets and spend most time paging. Admitting more processes shrinks each one's share further. The cure is to lower the degree of multiprogramming.$e$, 20, 5, now(), now()),
    ('2ec5e393-ae3b-5a44-b229-9d6ef629597e', '1507bec6-b75f-59a2-af91-52fb3dd32e4d', $q$What is the key conceptual difference between a mutex and a binary semaphore?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["A mutex has an owner: the thread that locked it is the one that should unlock it", "A mutex can count to 10, a binary semaphore cannot", "A binary semaphore can only be used by one process ever", "There is no difference of any kind"]$o$, $a$A mutex has an owner: the thread that locked it is the one that should unlock it$a$, $e$A mutex is for mutual exclusion and has ownership. A binary semaphore is a signaling tool: any thread may release it, even one that never acquired it. Both hold values 0 or 1, which is why they are confused.$e$, 10, 1, now(), now()),
    ('3b46decc-b384-5655-afe3-f07ea108f9e5', '1507bec6-b75f-59a2-af91-52fb3dd32e4d', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Semaphore s = new Semaphore(2);
        System.out.println(s.tryAcquire());
        System.out.println(s.tryAcquire());
        System.out.println(s.tryAcquire());
        s.release();
        System.out.println(s.availablePermits());
    }
}
$q$, 'PREDICT_OUTPUT', $o$["true / true / true / 0", "true / true / false / 1", "true / false / false / 1", "true / true / false / 2"]$o$, $a$true / true / false / 1$a$, $e$The semaphore starts with 2 permits, so two tryAcquire calls succeed and the third fails without blocking. release() returns one permit, so availablePermits() is 1.$e$, 15, 2, now(), now()),
    ('b9aee28e-0219-5be4-970d-01d5d3309c2a', '1507bec6-b75f-59a2-af91-52fb3dd32e4d', $q$A server may hold at most 5 simultaneous database connections. Extra requests should wait their turn. What do you use?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["A mutex around the whole request", "A boolean flag checked in a loop", "A counting semaphore initialized to 5", "A binary semaphore initialized to 0"]$o$, $a$A counting semaphore initialized to 5$a$, $e$Each request acquires a permit and releases it when done, so up to 5 run at once and the rest block. A mutex would allow only one at a time, and a semaphore starting at 0 would block everyone.$e$, 15, 3, now(), now()),
    ('2b0bb8d0-5c84-5fd2-ab7a-67f2f981ddb4', '1507bec6-b75f-59a2-af91-52fb3dd32e4d', $q$Two threads each run count++ one thousand times on a shared int with no synchronization. Why can the final value be less than 2000?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["Java limits an int to 1000 increments", "The JVM runs only one thread at a time", "count++ is atomic, so the result is always 2000", "count++ is read, add, write; interleaved steps can overwrite each other's updates"]$o$, $a$count++ is read, add, write; interleaved steps can overwrite each other's updates$a$, $e$Both threads can read the same old value, each add 1 and write back the same result, losing one increment. This is a race condition. Wrapping the increment in a mutex makes the three steps one critical section.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
    ('1fb5f692-bafb-5e54-9582-dac56419b2ab', '1507bec6-b75f-59a2-af91-52fb3dd32e4d', $q$Thread B must not run step 2 until thread A has finished step 1. Which semaphore setup enforces this?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["Initial value 1; A acquires it first, B releases it", "Initial value 0; A releases it after step 1, B acquires it before step 2", "Initial value 0; B releases it after step 1, A acquires it", "Initial value 5; both threads acquire it before starting"]$o$, $a$Initial value 0; A releases it after step 1, B acquires it before step 2$a$, $e$A semaphore at 0 makes B block on acquire until A signals with release. Starting at 1 or more lets B pass immediately, so the ordering would not be guaranteed.$e$, 20, 5, now(), now()),
    ('f2f72c4d-0bcc-5b57-89b2-4f98e426468e', 'e305fca6-ff71-5933-b412-c23fd68f73dc', $q$A bounded buffer uses semaphores empty (N), full (0) and mutex (1). A producer wrongly runs wait(mutex) BEFORE wait(empty). What can happen when the buffer is full?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["The producer overwrites the oldest item", "Deadlock: the producer holds the mutex while waiting for space, so no consumer can enter to free a slot", "Nothing, the order of waits does not matter", "The consumer produces the missing items itself"]$o$, $a$Deadlock: the producer holds the mutex while waiting for space, so no consumer can enter to free a slot$a$, $e$The producer sleeps on empty while holding mutex, and a consumer needs mutex to remove an item and signal empty. The counting semaphore must be waited on first, then the mutex.$e$, 15, 1, now(), now()),
    ('2d358e80-e647-599b-a679-7df5b4c7f23b', 'e305fca6-ff71-5933-b412-c23fd68f73dc', $q$In the classic readers-writers solution that gives readers priority, which problem can occur?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["Readers can starve forever", "Two writers can write at the same time", "Readers and a writer can read and write together", "Writers can starve if readers keep arriving"]$o$, $a$Writers can starve if readers keep arriving$a$, $e$New readers are admitted whenever any reader is active, so a waiting writer may never see the data unused. Mutual exclusion between writers and readers is still preserved.$e$, 15, 2, now(), now()),
    ('25d1c833-38ba-56b3-8a40-e674fc3106a8', 'e305fca6-ff71-5933-b412-c23fd68f73dc', $q$Which of these is NOT one of the four necessary conditions for deadlock?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["Mutual exclusion", "Hold and wait", "Resources can be preempted", "Circular wait"]$o$, $a$Resources can be preempted$a$, $e$The condition is no preemption: a resource can be released only voluntarily by its holder. If resources could be taken away, deadlock could be broken, so preemption is the opposite of a requirement.$e$, 20, 3, now(), now()),
    ('dcde8b84-962d-5522-aa38-739fab3395cb', 'e305fca6-ff71-5933-b412-c23fd68f73dc', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean[] taken = new boolean[5];
        boolean[] hasFirst = new boolean[5];
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            int first = (i == 4) ? 0 : i;
            if (!taken[first]) { taken[first] = true; hasFirst[i] = true; }
        }
        int eating = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            int second = (i == 4) ? 4 : (i + 1) % 5;
            if (hasFirst[i] && !taken[second]) { taken[second] = true; eating++; }
        }
        System.out.println(eating);
    }
}
$q$, 'PREDICT_OUTPUT', $o$["0", "1", "2", "5"]$o$, $a$1$a$, $e$Philosophers 0 to 3 take forks 0 to 3, while philosopher 4 is blocked on fork 0. Only philosopher 3 finds its second fork (4) free, so one eats. Breaking the symmetry removes the circular wait.$e$, 20, 4, now(), now()),
    ('c0992b3f-58ce-5a85-b1d7-f6365cbb8d7e', 'e305fca6-ff71-5933-b412-c23fd68f73dc', $q$Five philosophers each grab the left fork and then wait for the right one, and the table deadlocks. Which fix is guaranteed to prevent deadlock?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["Allow at most four philosophers to try to pick up forks at the same time", "After a failed attempt, put the fork down and retry after a random delay", "Give each philosopher a bigger plate", "Add a sixth philosopher to the table"]$o$, $a$Allow at most four philosophers to try to pick up forks at the same time$a$, $e$With at most 4 competing for 5 forks, at least one philosopher can always get both forks, so circular wait cannot form. Random back-off only makes deadlock unlikely and can livelock or starve someone.$e$, 20, 5, now(), now()),
    ('e927a3d3-98ff-53a7-b024-fe301d3d2d2f', 'ab82aa4c-c69e-5391-8471-90bb2893f650', $q$Which OSI layer is responsible for routing packets between different networks using IP addresses?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["Data link layer", "Network layer", "Transport layer", "Session layer"]$o$, $a$Network layer$a$, $e$The network layer (layer 3) handles logical addressing and routing across networks. The data link layer is tempting, but it only moves frames between devices on the same local link using MAC addresses.$e$, 10, 1, now(), now()),
    ('24504977-dc3c-5df7-9f56-368a73eb604a', 'ab82aa4c-c69e-5391-8471-90bb2893f650', $q$In the four-layer TCP/IP model, where do protocols such as HTTP and DNS live?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["Application layer", "Transport layer", "Internet layer", "Link layer"]$o$, $a$Application layer$a$, $e$HTTP and DNS are application protocols, so they sit in the TCP/IP application layer. TCP and UDP are the transport layer, and IP is the internet layer.$e$, 10, 2, now(), now()),
    ('c6ef2a07-2d7c-5491-8de6-411aa30f8351', 'ab82aa4c-c69e-5391-8471-90bb2893f650', $q$Two PCs plugged into the same switch cannot talk, and the link light on one network card is off. Which layer should you check first?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["Application layer (browser settings)", "Transport layer (TCP ports)", "Physical layer (cable, port, card)", "Presentation layer (encryption format)"]$o$, $a$Physical layer (cable, port, card)$a$, $e$With no link light, the problem is almost certainly at the bottom of the stack: cable, port or NIC. Troubleshooting from the physical layer upward avoids wasting time on software layers that cannot work without a link.$e$, 15, 3, now(), now()),
    ('dd852e2e-b26a-57d4-a8e6-abebacb3a418', 'ab82aa4c-c69e-5391-8471-90bb2893f650', $q$What is the unit of data called at the OSI data link layer?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["Segment", "Packet", "Bit", "Frame"]$o$, $a$Frame$a$, $e$The data link layer wraps data into frames with MAC addresses. A segment belongs to TCP at the transport layer and a packet belongs to IP at the network layer.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
    ('0b2537ae-f3ae-5fef-ae95-8ff496cdcd5b', 'ab82aa4c-c69e-5391-8471-90bb2893f650', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

