$q$, 'PREDICT_OUTPUT', $o$["TCP[IP[ETH[HELLO]]]", "ETH[IP[TCP[HELLO]]]", "IP[TCP[ETH[HELLO]]]", "ETH[TCP[IP[HELLO]]]"]$o$, $a$ETH[IP[TCP[HELLO]]]$a$, $e$Encapsulation adds headers as data moves down: transport first (TCP), then network (IP), then link (ETH), so the outermost wrapper is the lowest layer. The receiver peels them off in the reverse order.$e$, 20, 5, now(), now()),
    ('45dd3d85-6848-5f24-86d3-f95ef9ff4b8b', 'fa290d3c-af7d-57c9-879c-4e5a75b294a4', $q$In the address 192.168.1.0/26, what does the /26 tell you?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["The subnet holds exactly 26 hosts", "There are 26 subnets in the network", "The first 26 bits identify the network", "The last 26 bits identify the network"]$o$, $a$The first 26 bits identify the network$a$, $e$The prefix length is the number of leading bits that form the network part; here 32 - 26 = 6 bits remain for hosts. It is not a host or subnet count, and it counts from the left, not the right.$e$, 10, 1, now(), now()),
    ('6a3aa8ee-2ca9-58c5-b9b3-d282cbbbafd2', 'fa290d3c-af7d-57c9-879c-4e5a75b294a4', $q$Which of these is a private (RFC 1918) IPv4 address?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["172.20.5.9", "172.32.0.1", "192.169.1.1", "11.0.0.1"]$o$, $a$172.20.5.9$a$, $e$The private block 172.16.0.0 to 172.31.255.255 contains 172.20.5.9. 172.32.0.1 and 192.169.1.1 look similar but sit just outside the private ranges.$e$, 10, 2, now(), now()),
    ('fad6b76a-e006-51a3-9049-9eb998e7d616', 'fa290d3c-af7d-57c9-879c-4e5a75b294a4', $q$An office VLAN needs room for 50 devices. Which prefix gives the smallest subnet that still fits them?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["/27", "/25", "/28", "/26"]$o$, $a$/26$a$, $e$A /26 leaves 6 host bits: 64 addresses, 62 usable after network and broadcast. /27 gives only 30 usable, too few, while /25 gives 126 and wastes space.$e$, 15, 3, now(), now()),
    ('0415e2f4-54c9-51de-810a-e1ffad1df267', 'fa290d3c-af7d-57c9-879c-4e5a75b294a4', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

public class Main {
    static int usable(int prefix) {
        return (1 << (32 - prefix)) - 2;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(usable(28));
        System.out.println(usable(30));
    }
}
