}
$q$, 'PREDICT_OUTPUT', $o$["8 / 1 1 2 2 2 3 4 4", "3 / 1 2 3", "4 / 1 2 3 4", "5 / 1 2 3 4 4"]$o$, $a$4 / 1 2 3 4$a$, $e$Pointer w marks the next write slot and only advances when a new value differs from the last kept one, so the distinct values 1 2 3 4 are packed to the front and w ends at 4. Printing the whole array would show leftovers, but the loop only prints the first w items.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
    ('8273acea-bf2f-5dba-a37a-65678cf2c653', '6629ac7c-4deb-52c8-b77a-3ac96ac9a77f', $q$An UNSORTED array needs a pair-sum check using sorting plus two pointers. What is the overall time complexity?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["O(n)", "O(n log n)", "O(n^2)", "O(log n)"]$o$, $a$O(n log n)$a$, $e$The two-pointer sweep is O(n), but sorting costs O(n log n) and dominates. Thinking the whole thing is O(n) forgets the sort that makes the sweep valid.$e$, 20, 5, now(), now()),
    ('6010f859-de47-5edb-bc33-2f30412d3110', 'abb2e78f-81af-5423-aa76-75fed9b4b9c3', $q$What is the main idea that makes a fixed-size sliding window fast?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["Sorting the array first", "Reuse the previous window's result by adding the entering element and removing the leaving one", "Using recursion to split the array in half", "Storing every possible window in a hash map"]$o$, $a$Reuse the previous window's result by adding the entering element and removing the leaving one$a$, $e$Neighbouring windows overlap almost completely, so updating the result in O(1) per step turns O(n*k) into O(n). Sorting would destroy the order that defines a window.$e$, 10, 1, now(), now()),
    ('b47912b0-faed-5dcc-8482-7457efaf3655', 'abb2e78f-81af-5423-aa76-75fed9b4b9c3', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] a = {2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2, 9};
        int k = 3, sum = 0, best = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
            sum += a[i];
            if (i >= k) sum -= a[i - k];
            if (i >= k - 1) best = Math.max(best, sum);
        }
        System.out.println(best);
    }
}
$q$, 'PREDICT_OUTPUT', $o$["12", "9", "14", "15"]$o$, $a$14$a$, $e$The window sums are 8, 7, 9, 6 and finally 3+2+9 = 14, so best ends as 14. Reusing sum by adding the new element and subtracting the one that left gives each of them in O(1).$e$, 10, 2, now(), now()),
    ('7ec03db6-8780-5931-a90c-092940bcbf8c', 'abb2e78f-81af-5423-aa76-75fed9b4b9c3', $q$You need the length of the longest substring of a string that contains no repeated character. Which approach is best?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["Check every substring with a set, O(n^3)", "Sort the characters and count the distinct ones", "Expand a window to the right and shrink it from the left whenever a repeat appears", "Split the string at every repeated character and take the shortest piece"]$o$, $a$Expand a window to the right and shrink it from the left whenever a repeat appears$a$, $e$A variable-size window keeps a repeat-free segment and only moves forward, giving O(n). Brute force works but is far too slow, and sorting loses the contiguity the question depends on.$e$, 15, 3, now(), now()),
    ('f9aea135-3730-5647-9ced-ab3e61cbd5ef', 'abb2e78f-81af-5423-aa76-75fed9b4b9c3', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] a = {3, 1, 2, 1, 3, 4};
        int limit = 8, left = 0, sum = 0, best = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < a.length; right++) {
            sum += a[right];
            while (sum > limit) sum -= a[left++];
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
        }
        System.out.println(best);
    }
}
$q$, 'PREDICT_OUTPUT', $o$["4", "5", "3", "2"]$o$, $a$4$a$, $e$With all-positive numbers the window grows until its sum exceeds 8, then shrinks from the left. The longest window with sum at most 8 is 1 2 1 3 with sum 7, length 4; 5 would need sum 8 plus another element.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
    ('1056a8e1-e0c7-5827-ba6b-94e153cd00d8', 'abb2e78f-81af-5423-aa76-75fed9b4b9c3', $q$A variable-size window algorithm has a while loop nested inside its for loop. Why can it still run in O(n)?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["The inner loop always runs at most twice", "Each element enters the window once and leaves at most once, so total pointer moves are at most 2n", "Nested loops are always linear when the array is sorted", "The inner loop runs only when the array has duplicates"]$o$, $a$Each element enters the window once and leaves at most once, so total pointer moves are at most 2n$a$, $e$Both pointers only move forward, so the combined work of the loops is bounded by about 2n, not n squared. Counting loops nested is not the same as multiplying their counts when the inner pointer never resets.$e$, 20, 5, now(), now()),
    ('a9ee527d-0247-5c17-ac2e-748fce19d3d1', 'cd3f08b2-9cdc-5a3d-8d19-b32afa2260bb', $q$A recursive method has no base case. What happens when it is called?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["It throws StackOverflowError after the call stack fills up", "It returns the default value 0", "It loops forever using constant memory", "The compiler refuses to compile it"]$o$, $a$It throws StackOverflowError after the call stack fills up$a$, $e$Every call adds a stack frame and none ever returns, so the finite stack runs out. Java does not optimise away recursion, so it does not run forever in constant memory, and the compiler cannot detect a missing base case.$e$, 10, 1, now(), now()),
    ('56586a8a-0474-52b3-89fb-1f04c57bc845', 'cd3f08b2-9cdc-5a3d-8d19-b32afa2260bb', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

