$q$, 'PREDICT_OUTPUT', $o$["3 / 2 / 1 / 1 / 2 / 3", "1 / 2 / 3 / 10 / 20 / 30", "3 / 2 / 1 / 10 / 20 / 30", "30 / 20 / 10 / 3 / 2 / 1"]$o$, $a$3 / 2 / 1 / 10 / 20 / 30$a$, $e$The first println runs on the way down (3, 2, 1) and the second runs as the calls return, in reverse order of calling: 10, 20, 30. The tempting reversed first half forgets that the call happens after the first print.$e$, 10, 2, now(), now()),
    ('f36eef28-a573-5783-9ca2-9bbb19f16023', 'cd3f08b2-9cdc-5a3d-8d19-b32afa2260bb', $q$You must list every possible ordering of 4 distinct items. Which technique is the natural fit?$q$, 'CHOOSE_APPROACH', $o$["Binary search on the answer", "Backtracking: choose an unused item, recurse, then unchoose it", "A sliding window over the items", "A single for loop that swaps neighbours once"]$o$, $a$Backtracking: choose an unused item, recurse, then unchoose it$a$, $e$Permutations are built position by position, so you need to try each unused item, explore, and undo the choice to try the next. A sliding window or one pass of swaps cannot enumerate all orderings, and binary search needs a monotonic answer.$e$, 15, 3, now(), now()),
    ('53f1190c-970e-5f06-8c22-82068e9c72a1', 'cd3f08b2-9cdc-5a3d-8d19-b32afa2260bb', $q$What does this program print? (Lines are shown separated by " / ".)

public class Main {
    static int[] a = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};

    static int count(int i, int rem) {
        if (rem == 0) return 1;
        if (rem < 0 || i == a.length) return 0;
        return count(i + 1, rem - a[i]) + count(i + 1, rem);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(count(0, 5));
    }
}
$q$, 'PREDICT_OUTPUT', $o$["2", "4", "7", "3"]$o$, $a$3$a$, $e$The subsets of {1..5} that sum to 5 are {5}, {1,4} and {2,3}. Choosing 'take or skip' at each index explores them all; 2 forgets the single-element subset {5}.$e$, 15, 4, now(), now()),
    ('d1e43019-a7d5-5e84-955c-237698cda63b', 'cd3f08b2-9cdc-5a3d-8d19-b32afa2260bb', $q$Roughly how much work does it take to generate all permutations of n distinct items?$q$, 'MCQ', $o$["About n^2 steps", "About 2^n steps", "About n! permutations, each costing about n to build or copy", "About n log n steps"]$o$, $a$About n! permutations, each costing about n to build or copy$a$, $e$There are n! permutations and just writing each one out costs n, so the work is on the order of n * n!. 2^n counts subsets, not orderings, and polynomial answers are far too small.$e$, 20, 5, now(), now())
